# Strike rule — [N] — [short name]

> A strike rule is a one-paragraph constraint derived from observed failures. **Don't write a rule until the same failure pattern has happened 3+ times.**

## 1. Date

[YYYY-MM-DD when the rule was created. The 3rd occurrence of the failure pattern is the trigger.]

## 2. Shape

The failure pattern, in one sentence.

> "When [X happens], the [agent/cron/skill] [does Y, which is wrong]."

**The shape is the abstract pattern, not the specific incident.** "When the user pastes an X link, the agent narrates the tool calls in chat" is too specific. "When the agent has a multi-tool-call action to perform, the agent narrates each tool call" is the right shape.

## 3. Why

What does the rule prevent? 1-2 sentences.

> "Operator context window fills with narration instead of action. The actual work is still done, but the chat transcript is unreadable."

The "why" is **the consequence, not the failure.** The failure is "agent narrates." The consequence is "operator context window fills." The rule prevents the consequence.

## 4. What to do

The correct behavior. 1-2 sentences.

> "Agent does the action; chat only confirms with one sentence at the end."

The "what to do" is **specific and bounded.** It says what the agent should do, not what the agent should think about. If the agent has to interpret the rule, the rule is too vague.

## 5. Counter-example

The antipattern the rule is correcting. A real example from the operator's system, redacted if needed.

> "In the previous session, the agent narrated 5 tool calls in chat (read /path/to/file, then write to /path/to/output, then verify, etc.). The operator ran out of context room before the agent's actual work completed."

The counter-example is **real, not hypothetical.** If the rule has no real example, the rule isn't ready.

---

## Approval

- [ ] Failure pattern observed 3+ times (date and incident count documented)
- [ ] Shape is abstract (not specific to one incident)
- [ ] Why is the consequence (not the failure)
- [ ] What to do is specific and bounded
- [ ] Counter-example is real
- [ ] Rule fits in one paragraph
- [ ] Rule is operator-context (not portable)

Once all 7 boxes are checked, the rule is ready. File it in the rules section of your vault.
